===Etymology===
From {{proto|ine-pro|ḱwṓ|lang=lv}}, genitive ''*ḱunós'', ''*ḱunés'', from which Latvian {{term|so|tr=suo|lang=lv}}, {{term|suns|lang=lv}} (''so'' is now found only in one dialect as a word to call dogs). Cognates include {{etyl|lt|-}} {{term|šuo|šuõ|lang=lt}} (genitive {{term|šuns|šuñs|lang=lt}}, dialectal {{term|šunis|lang=lt}}), {{etyl|prg|-}} {{term|sunnis|sunis|lang=prg}}, {{proto|gem-pro|hundaz|lang=}} ({{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃|sc=Goth|tr=hunds|lang=got}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|hundr|lang=non}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|hundur|lang=is}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|hund|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|hunt|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|hund|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Hund|lang=de}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|hond|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|en|-}} {{term|hound|lang=en}}), {{etyl|ga|-}} {{term|cú|lang=ga}}, {{etyl|cy|-}} {{term|ci|lang=cy}}, plural {{term|cŵn|lang=cy}}, {{etyl|sa|-}} {{term|श्वन्|tr=śván|lang=sa}}, genitive ''śúnaḥ'', {{etyl|ae|-}} {{term|lang=ae|sc=Avst|𐬯𐬞𐬁|tr=spā}}, genitive plural ''sunam'', {{etyl|xcl|-}} {{term|շուն|tr=šun|lang=xcl}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|κύων|tr=kúōn|lang=grc}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|canis|lang=la}} ({{etyl|it|-}} {{term|cane|lang=it}}, {{etyl|pt|-}} {{term|cão|lang=pt}}, {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|chien|lang=fr}})
<ref>Karulis, Konstantīns. 1992, 2001. ''Latviešu etimoloģijas vārdnīca.'' Rīga: AVOTS. ISBN 9984700127.</ref>

